welcome to what's new in networking a  series to keep you up to date with  well what's new in networking this is  stephanie wong and today we'll be  covering our core  infrastructure updates we'll cover some  new shared vpc capabilities as well as  new mtu settings for interconnect so  stay tuned  [Music]  shared vpc provides the ability for  organizations to connect resources from  multiple service projects  to a common vpc this is amazing as it  provides  centralized control over networking  resources like subnets routes and  firewall rules  and it was great for vms with a single  nic but  it wasn't ideal for vms which require  nik 0  to be a non-shared vpc network this  means something like this was  not supported nik 0 would be tied to the  local vpc  while nick one would be assigned to the  shared vpc  to support this customers would have to  deploy proxies to front-end the  instances  which led to more management and  resource overhead  with this recent launch we now support  shared vpc  on a non-default nic now you can attach  a shared bpc to  any nic of an instance and this  eliminates the requirement of proxies  and gives more flexibility for  vm configurations and now on to  mtu mtu stands for maximum transmission  unit the size of the largest protocol  data unit that can be communicated in a  single network layer transaction  a larger mtu can bring greater  efficiency because each network packet  carries more data  while protocol overheads like headers  remain fixed  1500 bytes mtu is an industry standard  ipacket size  the default mtu for google cloud vpc is  1460 bytes  as of the end of 2020 you're now able to  increase the mtu  to 1500 bytes while this gave vms the  ability to exchange 1500 byte ip packets  with other vms  services or the public internet there  was still a discrepancy with cloud  interconnect  which provides direct physical  connections between your on-premise  network and  google's network prior to this launch  google cloud  interconnect mtu was set to 1440 bytes  with this new launch we now support  end-to-end 1500 byte ntu support  organizations can lift and shift or  support hybrid workloads  with strict mtu requirements  and that's what's new in networking for  core infrastructure  let us know in the comments if you think  these will accelerate and improve your  cloud journey  be sure to take a look at the links  below for details and guides on how to  use them  and stay tuned for the latest launches  in our next category on  what's new in networking  you
 

